A||||7||ThatRickGuy||Installing new bushings, ? about sleeve||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||11-21-2000||03:50 PM||||Hey guys, I just got my new poly bushings in, probrably going to work on them friday/saturday. but anyway, I searched the forum and I've heard a lot of talk about burning/freezing/muscling the old rubber bushings off the metal sleeves, yet my replacement prothane polys showed up with a metal sleeve in all of the bushings (front upper/lower control arm set) and I was if you had to get the rubber off to get the old sleeve off or the outter shell. And also, did prothane just start sending the sleeve with? because I noticed some other people talking about the same set and still trying to figure out how to get the old sleeve out.<P>Thanks! BTW, the 3.2 is kickin' butt. Not as nice as some of those 3.4s out there, but definately a big jump from the 2.8.||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||11-21-2000||05:59 PM||||I just replaced the upper bushings with new rubber ones.  The sleeve comes out with the rubber.  I just pounded with a drift on the inside and a chisel around the ring on the out side until the were out far enough to grab with a vise grip.  Be careful removing the nuts on the shaft if you got an 88. I broke one and they are not available anywhere except boneyards.  Have fun.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RNGCRSH||11-21-2000||06:25 PM||||The poly bushings do not have a metal sleeve around the outside. the old rubber bushings are pressed into the control arms and have a sleeve on the outside. you need to remove the rubber from the inside of the sleeve without distorting the pressed in sleeve if you pound on the rubber it will stretch the sleeve. use a propane torch and burn the rubber so it comes out easily, leaving the pressed in outer Sleeve. If you distort this sleeve then your suspension will be loose and will not be able to be aligned. also remember to grease the bushings when putting it all back together or it will squeek enough to drive you crazy.<P><BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||11-21-2000||11:21 PM||||Yes, that is correct.  Also, dont forget to grease the inner sleaves b4 pressing the bushing into the control arms!<P>I am finishing up my bushing install, and forgot to grease the inner sleaves on all my control arm bushings!<P>Ahhh, at least the outer bushing has been greesed <B>WELL</B>!<P>good luck!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||11-22-2000||07:57 AM||||Hey guys, thanks for the pointers. I picked up some extra grease so I should have plenty to goob the heck out of them. hopefully everything will come apart easily, altho I know the nuts on the ends are rusted to all heck, I guess it's a PB Blaster kinda job.<P>-Rick||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||11-22-2000||08:06 AM||||    Rick, don't burn a thing. It's an unneccessary mess. Use a drill and a 1/4 0r 5/16 drill bit, make 4 or 5 holes through the rubber bushing to the inside near to the center sleve as possible. Use a hammer and a drift pin and tap the center sleve out. Then use a screw driver and pry the rubber out, Don't worry, you wont be using enough pressure to distort anything. This proccess takes all of 3-5 minutes per bushing and is alot cleaner and safer that burning or freezing. You will have a little mess from the rubber shavings from the drill, no big deal. Give it a shot on one bushing, if you don't like it, BURN EM, BURN EM ALL!!!!!!<P> P.S. On the smaller bushings, use a smaller bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> Peace, BAM OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000006||bHooper||11-22-2000||10:35 AM||||i found it very easy to remove most of them by using a propane torch.  you heat up the sleaves with the torch (to the point where the rubber just starts to smoke inside), the press them out.  they will pop out real easy like.<P>Bob Englert loaned me a tool (i wouldnt call it a press, more of a vice with a special circular end) to press them out with... very easy!<P>i did have to take the upper control arms to the shop (do to size and shape of the a arm), and i haven't done the cradle yet.  but this should work fine for that.<P>good luck!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-22-2000||02:10 PM||||On the cradel I tore them up good with a drill and yanked out the chunks with pliers. There's not allot of room to work if the cradel is in the car. to finnish I used a torch to cook the rubber enough to make it easier to scrape out. Be carefull to protect brake/fuel lines from torch heat. Make temporarry heat sheilds and keep a water hose handy just in case.<P>If you scar up the inside of the cradel bushing shell with the drill it's no big deal <I>as long as you don't thin out the shell so it will crack.</I> These bushings don't rotate. Mine are installed with no grease at all and don't squeak even a little.<P>make sure you put anti sieze on the whole bolt that holds the front of the cradel. If that rusts into the new sleeve you'll never get it out. On the back bolts just coat the threads.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
